# a-wall-cut-from-rock — BACKLOG C6, and it is the TOP of a triple.
#
# `a-wall-cut-from-sand.keys`, `a-base-the-player-builds.keys` and this
# file are the same file except for ONE token on five lines — the
# palette index the band is painted with:
#
#     palette 4  →  the band is SAND    falls at 153
#     palette 5  →  the band is GRASS   falls at 174   (`@M050`)
#     palette 7  →  the band is ROCK    falls at 220   (`@M061`)
#
# Same wall, same core, same spawn, same wave list, same crew, same
# drive, same number of ticks spent before the wave — so every
# difference is the GROUND's and can be nothing else (`plans/12` § B7).
#
# ── ⚠⚠ What it is measuring ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# **A wall is as strong as the ground it was cut from.**  The crew do
# not haul (`build.loft` has a helper stand at the site and spend
# seconds), so the stone comes from where they are standing, and
# `damage.loft::footing_of` reads the sturdiest ground the wall
# touches.  The palette's SLOPE ladder is the hardness ladder — an
# angle of repose is how well a material holds itself up — and it had
# been carried since plan 01 with nothing reading it, exactly as `drop`
# was before BACKLOG C5.
#
# ⚠ **+90 ticks over a bare base against grass's +44** — twice the
# wall, for the same ten helper-seconds a hex, because the map put rock
# there.  ⚠⚠ And 220 is within a tick of the TRENCH's 221 (`@M059`),
# which is where the ceiling was priced: the best wall and a trench cost
# the same and buy the same clock, so choosing between them is about
# what a barrier DOES rather than which is stronger.
#
# ── ⚠⚠ And the honest limit: the MAP chose, not the player ───────
#
# Nothing here is a decision anybody made in play.  The band is
# authored rock, the wall goes where it always went, and the player got
# a better wall for free.  ⚠ **The footing is a map-authoring lever
# until a map puts the good ground somewhere awkward** — which is
# `PROGRESSION.md` § G3's racing line, and a change to `maps/` rather
# than to any code.

echo a wall cut from rock: the same wall, on ground worth twice as much

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 7
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80
# The band is rock from end to end — the ONE token, and the only thing
# that differs from `a-base-the-player-builds.keys`.
kind 8 0 rock

# The crew, sitting on the line the player is about to draw.
crew 5 -1
crew 5 1

# ── The build (or not) ──────────────────────────────────────────
park 5 -2
paint on
drive 5 2
tick 6
count orders 5 5
paint off

# Park clear of the line so the trail is not rubbed out, and let the
# crew work.  60 helper-seconds over two helpers is 30 s = 45 ticks.
drive 0 0
tick 50
count orders 0 0
count painted 80 80
# ⚠⚠ The whole band, hex by hex — because a wall with ONE gap in it
# buys nothing at all (`plans/12` § B7 measured a gate at +1 tick), so
# *five* is the number that matters rather than *some*.
kind 5 -2 wall
kind 5 -1 wall
kind 5 0 wall
kind 5 1 wall
kind 5 2 wall

# ⚠⚠ **The mechanic, read exactly, before a single robot arrives.**
#
# ⚠ A COLUMN is not a straight run on this lattice: constant `q` with
# `r` varying ZIGZAGS, because row parity flips which delta each
# direction index carries, so every middle hex is a turning pair and
# takes `BRACE_FULL` (`damage.loft` § "Straight" is a DIRECTION-INDEX
# opposition).  A wall a player drags down the screen is a
# crinkle-crankle wall and is stronger for it.
#
# So the middle of this column is the full `wall_hp` times its footing:
# **100 on grass, 50 on sand, 200 on rock.**  This one is 200.
hp 5 0 200 200


# ── The waves — identical to `an-undefended-base` ───────────────
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ **130 against 174 — the wall the player DREW is worth +44 ticks,
# a third of the base's life** (`@M050`).  Same ground, same core, same
# spawn, same wave list, same crew, same drive, same ticks spent — one
# token apart.
fall 900
ticks 216 224

# ⚠ Nothing died on the way, exactly as in the grass version — a wall
# buys ticks, never income.
count alive 13 13
snap c6_a_wall_cut_from_rock
